                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Despite its relative high price and its average title, Skipper manages still to be a very nice and addictive puzzle game in my view.\nThe game is quite poorly made for what concerns the user interface, you'll keep on seeing all the white windows and you won't have any option besides those related to effects and music.. so, despite being realized in Unity, the game does not make great use of the engine's potential.\nThis said, the game itself as I was saying doesn't even include an innovative mechanic - it's one of those puzzle games in which you control a cube in a 3D environment, and you're asked to step on every tile and, at the same time, picking up the colored cubes (in this case of course) and putting automatically them down when stepping on the colored correspondant tile.\nEverything's done automatically and most of all you can stack the cubes, meaning that you have to think more on the path you'll make (you can't retrace your steps, so a tile already walked on becomes basically a wall and they will be marked with a black dot) rather than a strategy to pick up and put down the different color cubes.\nThe game features more than 30 levels, and already during the first ones you'll have to deal with multiple cubes - it will definitely require some time to be completed, but the save function is automatic and you can always log off and pick up from the level selection screen where you left.\nI highly doubt that this game will have the trading cards that promises in the future, but it won't even be ever taken down from the Steam store and it's a very nice addition to your puzzle library if you're into this genre.\nAs I said, it will definitely require some strategy and even if the levels and the free expansions don't offer hours and hours of playtime, every level it's still hard and entertaining, always challenging and, if played casually for some days, definitely funny.\nThe soundtrack and the minimalistic graphic/user interface unfortunately aren't that great, but still the game can be completely played with the WASD keys and the R one to restart without many problems, even with the small video flaws it has.\nThe lack of a tutorial isn't really important here - the game features just that mechanic of painting tiles, collecting colored cubes and reaching the end so really you just have to play with your movement keys and it's ok until the end :)\nDefinitely recommended to all puzzle lovers: the overall game mechanics are nothing new at all, but still games like these are realized in a beautiful way for what concerns the pure gameplay and they provide entertaining and challenging hours!",
